PART H — FINANCIAL REPORTING

19. DAILY FINANCIAL STATEMENT

Create a live daily financial report.

Display:

- Opening balance per account.
- Money received.
- Earned income.
- Loan proceeds.
- Money sent.
- Actual expenditure.
- Loan repayments.
- Cash withdrawals.
- Fees.
- Refunds and reversals.
- Internal transfers.
- Pending events.
- Unclassified events.
- Closing balance per account.
- Consolidated financial position.
- Reconciliation differences.

Make the difference between money movement and actual spending obvious.

A transfer is not income merely because it enters another account.

A withdrawal is not proof that money was consumed.

A loan is not earned income.

A refund is not new ordinary income.

20. DAILY NET FLOW DEFINITIONS

Do not use a single ambiguous figure called "net income".

Show clearly defined measures:

Net external cash flow: External credits minus external debits, using clearly documented classifications.

Net earned income: Recognized earned income minus recognized expenses, where the reporting definition supports that comparison.

Net account movement: Total change in the selected account balances, including the effect of transfers and other posted movements.

Actual expenditure: Expenses recognized through the accounting ledger, excluding internal transfers and unspent cash withdrawals.

Show loan proceeds, loan repayments and other liability movements separately.

Never combine unrelated financial measures into one misleading total.

21. HISTORICAL ANALYTICS

